EARLY EDITIONS OF A LANDMARK CHESS MANUAL by French composer and chess master AndrÃ© Danican Philidor (1726â1795), widely regarded as the best chess player of his age. A chess opening, an endgame position, and a checkmate method are all named after him. His work Analyse du jeu des Ãchecs was considered to be a huge advance in chess knowledge, hence becoming a standard chess manual for at least a century. First printed in 1749 (ESTC lists 3 issues printed that year), it went through more than 100 editions; German, Danish, and English translations all appeared in the eighteenth century, ensuring the text's widespread influence. The work analysis nine different types of game openings, and includes Philidor's famous phrase "[les pions] sont l'Ã¢me des Echecs" ("pawns are the soul of chess").
